WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.420
So what did the property look like when you first got there?

2
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.780
Nothing but woods, you know.

3
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.980
A lot of trees, brush.

4
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.740
Was that how most of the properties looked when you got here?

5
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:12.520
Yeah, yeah.

6
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.880
We had . . . They had a house on side of us that a guy owned,

7
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.760
and then we had the Thibodeauxs owned the property across from us.

8
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:29.140
But behind us they had a house that were there, but it had torn . . . It was pretty much destroyed.

9
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.100
I don’t know what happened to it.

10
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.580
But all the way to the . . . Everything was pretty much still wooded.

11
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:45.280
They had a few houses on Third Street, but across from us is pretty much the same that . . . like it was in 1980.

12
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.880
Are your neighbors still the same folks?

13
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.940
No, they . . . Everybody moved out around ’96.

14
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.840
So who were your neighbors when you first kind of moved into that area?

15
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.640
The Thibodeaux family.

16
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.520
It was like six girls and one guy.

17
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.940
That’s the first family that I met when I was in Mossville.

18
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.980
I can remember the Bryants.

19
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.100
You know, they had a lot of families here.

20
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:17.440
That’s the thing that kind of . . . why I wanted to do this because we still own some . . . the property over there.

21
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.520
And in ’96 they had the lawsuit and all with that.

22
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:27.960
Then they bought a lot of people out, but my grandmother didn’t want to . . . She’s old school and she didn’t want to sell.

23
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.720
Then when she passed away, my mom and dad they didn’t . . . They didn’t want to sell it.

24
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.740
So we never got around to selling the property, but my little boy has a horse on the property now.

25
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.680
And when we’re out there feeding the horse we always see old people that used to live in that area come by and say,

26
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.980
“Man I sure miss being back here.”

27
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.700
So and that’s kind of why I wanted to have a record of it.

28
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:57.080
Because I’m . . . The way things going, eventually Sasol is going to buy us out too,

29
00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.640
so . . . And I just wanted a record of that: we were there at one time.

30
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.620
Because I know we’re not going to be able to drive back there once they buy it out. [laughing]
